1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,308
Let's get right into it. Number 10, The "Free Trial"

2
00:00:04,308 --> 00:00:05,170
That Isn't.

3
00:00:05,170 --> 00:00:08,332
You're scrolling, minding your own business, and then BAM! An

4
00:00:08,332 --> 00:00:11,495
ad for a revolutionary new product. Maybe it’s a weight

5
00:00:11,495 --> 00:00:14,657
loss supplement that promises you’ll look like a Greek god

6
00:00:14,657 --> 00:00:17,820
in a week, or a skincare cream that swears it’ll

7
00:00:17,820 --> 00:00:20,982
erase 20 years from your face faster than you can

8
00:00:20,982 --> 00:00:24,145
say "botox." The catch? There isn't one, they say! Just

9
00:00:24,145 --> 00:00:27,307
pay shipping for a "free trial." Sounds harmless, right? You

10
00:00:27,307 --> 00:00:30,470
punch in your credit card details, thinking, "What's the worst

11
00:00:30,470 --> 00:00:33,633
that could happen?" Well, the worst, my friend, is that

12
00:00:33,633 --> 00:00:36,795
you've just signed up for a recurring subscription that costs

13
00:00:36,795 --> 00:00:39,958
more than your monthly rent, and canceling it is harder

14
00:00:39,958 --> 00:00:43,120
than getting a straight answer from a politician. These companies

15
00:00:43,120 --> 00:00:46,283
are masters of fine print and dark patterns. They hide

16
00:00:46,283 --> 00:00:49,445
the auto-renewal clause in a font so small it needs

17
00:00:49,445 --> 00:00:52,608
a microscope, or bury it under layers of click-through agreements.

18
00:00:52,608 --> 00:00:55,770
Before you know it, you're getting charged $99.99 every month

19
00:00:55,770 --> 00:00:58,933
for a product you tried once, hated, and then promptly

20
00:00:58,933 --> 00:01:02,096
forgot about. It's the digital equivalent of that gym membership

21
00:01:02,096 --> 00:01:05,258
you signed up for in January and completely abandoned by

22
00:01:05,258 --> 00:01:08,421
February, except this one keeps bleeding your bank account dry

23
00:01:08,421 --> 00:01:11,583
without you even having to feel guilty about not working

24
00:01:11,583 --> 00:01:11,900
out.

25
00:01:11,900 --> 00:01:15,550
Number 9, The Fake Tech Support Call.

26
00:01:15,550 --> 00:01:18,935
Ring, ring! Your phone buzzes. You answer, and a stern-sounding

27
00:01:18,935 --> 00:01:22,321
voice with an exotic accent informs you that they're from

28
00:01:22,321 --> 00:01:25,707
"Microsoft" or "Apple" and your computer is riddled with viruses,

29
00:01:25,707 --> 00:01:29,093
or perhaps it's sending out spam emails faster than a

30
00:01:29,093 --> 00:01:32,479
politician sends out fundraising requests. They tell you your digital

31
00:01:32,479 --> 00:01:35,865
life is in peril, and only they can save it.

32
00:01:35,865 --> 00:01:39,251
All you need to do is grant them remote access

33
00:01:39,251 --> 00:01:42,637
to your computer, and maybe, just maybe, buy some extremely

34
00:01:42,637 --> 00:01:46,023
overpriced "protection software" or pay a "diagnostic fee" with gift

35
00:01:46,023 --> 00:01:49,409
cards. Now, a rational person might think, "Wait, why would

36
00:01:49,409 --> 00:01:52,795
Microsoft call *me*? And how do they even know I

37
00:01:52,795 --> 00:01:56,181
have a problem?" But in that moment of panic, when

38
00:01:56,181 --> 00:01:59,567
your digital photos and bank accounts feel threatened, logic often

39
00:01:59,567 --> 00:02:02,953
takes a vacation. People, these companies do not cold-call you

40
00:02:02,953 --> 00:02:06,339
about your computer problems. It's like your car dealership calling

41
00:02:06,339 --> 00:02:09,725
to say your tires are flat without you having told

42
00:02:09,725 --> 00:02:13,111
them anything about your car – it just doesn't happen.

43
00:02:13,111 --> 00:02:16,497
They'll install actual malware, steal your personal data, or simply

44
00:02:16,497 --> 00:02:19,883
charge you an exorbitant amount for fixing a problem that

45
00:02:19,883 --> 00:02:23,269
never existed. It’s a classic, but it still reels in

46
00:02:23,269 --> 00:02:25,640
victims like fish on a Friday night.

47
00:02:25,640 --> 00:02:28,650
Number 8, The Grandparent Scam.

48
00:02:28,650 --> 00:02:32,267
This one is truly despicable because it preys on the

49
00:02:32,267 --> 00:02:35,885
love and concern people have for their family. Imagine this:

50
00:02:35,885 --> 00:02:39,503
your phone rings, and it's a frantic voice, muffled or

51
00:02:39,503 --> 00:02:43,120
distorted, claiming to be your grandchild. They're in terrible trouble

52
00:02:43,120 --> 00:02:46,738
– they've been arrested, they're stuck in a foreign country,

53
00:02:46,738 --> 00:02:50,356
they've been in an accident, or they need emergency surgery.

54
00:02:50,356 --> 00:02:53,974
The kicker? They beg you not to tell their parents,

55
00:02:53,974 --> 00:02:57,591
often saying something like, "Grampa, please, I'm so ashamed, don't

56
00:02:57,591 --> 00:03:01,209
tell Mom and Dad!" They need money, immediately, usually through

57
00:03:01,209 --> 00:03:04,827
wire transfer, gift cards, or cryptocurrency – methods that are

58
00:03:04,827 --> 00:03:08,445
hard to trace. The urgency, the emotional plea, and the

59
00:03:08,445 --> 00:03:12,062
implied secrecy short-circuit critical thinking. Many grandparents, caught off guard

60
00:03:12,062 --> 00:03:15,680
and desperate to help, don't take the few seconds it

61
00:03:15,680 --> 00:03:19,298
would require to call their grandchild directly or contact their

62
00:03:19,298 --> 00:03:22,916
parents to verify the story. It’s like a horror movie

63
00:03:22,916 --> 00:03:26,533
where the monster is your own loving heart, tricked into

64
00:03:26,533 --> 00:03:30,151
giving away your life savings to a stranger. Always, always

65
00:03:30,151 --> 00:03:33,769
verify. A quick call to another family member can save

66
00:03:33,769 --> 00:03:35,940
you thousands and a broken heart.

67
00:03:35,940 --> 00:03:39,590
Number 7, The Lottery or Sweepstakes Scam.

68
00:03:39,590 --> 00:03:43,253
Congratulations! You've just won ten million dollars in a lottery

69
00:03:43,253 --> 00:03:46,916
you never entered, or a sweepstakes you’ve never heard of!

70
00:03:46,916 --> 00:03:50,579
All you have to do is pay a small "processing

71
00:03:50,579 --> 00:03:54,242
fee," "tax," or "customs charge" to release your winnings. Seriously,

72
00:03:54,242 --> 00:03:57,905
how many times has this played out? It's the equivalent

73
00:03:57,905 --> 00:04:01,568
of someone telling you they've found a pot of gold

74
00:04:01,568 --> 00:04:05,231
at the end of a rainbow, but you need to

75
00:04:05,231 --> 00:04:08,894
pay them for the shovel. The scam relies on pure,

76
00:04:08,894 --> 00:04:12,557
unadulterated greed and the fantasy of instant wealth. No legitimate

77
00:04:12,557 --> 00:04:16,220
lottery or sweepstakes will ever ask you to pay money

78
00:04:16,220 --> 00:04:19,883
upfront to receive your winnings. Think about it: if you've

79
00:04:19,883 --> 00:04:23,546
actually won millions, they can just deduct the "fee" from

80
00:04:23,546 --> 00:04:27,209
your prize money. The fact that they can't, or won't,

81
00:04:27,209 --> 00:04:30,872
is your first and only red flag. These scammers often

82
00:04:30,872 --> 00:04:34,535
send official-looking letters, complete with fake seals and impressive letterheads,

83
00:04:34,535 --> 00:04:38,198
making it seem incredibly legitimate. But the moment they ask

84
00:04:38,198 --> 00:04:41,861
you to send money via wire transfer, gift cards, or

85
00:04:41,861 --> 00:04:45,524
even bitcoin, for your "winnings," you can be 100% sure

86
00:04:45,524 --> 00:04:49,187
it’s a scam. Your millions are as real as my

87
00:04:49,187 --> 00:04:49,920
pet unicorn.

88
00:04:49,920 --> 00:04:53,335
Ah, the phishing email. The cockroach of the internet –

89
00:04:53,335 --> 00:04:56,750
ugly, annoying, and seemingly impossible to get rid of. You

90
00:04:56,750 --> 00:05:00,165
know the drill: an email lands in your inbox, seemingly

91
00:05:00,165 --> 00:05:03,581
from your bank, PayPal, Netflix, or even your boss. It

92
00:05:03,581 --> 00:05:06,996
says there's been "suspicious activity" on your account, your password

93
00:05:06,996 --> 00:05:10,411
needs to be "reset immediately," or there's an "urgent invoice"

94
00:05:10,411 --> 00:05:13,826
waiting. It looks legitimate, the logo is there, the tone

95
00:05:13,826 --> 00:05:17,242
is urgent, and there's a convenient link to "verify your

96
00:05:17,242 --> 00:05:20,657
details." Click that link, and you're not going to your

97
00:05:20,657 --> 00:05:24,072
bank's website. You're going to a meticulously crafted replica designed

98
00:05:24,072 --> 00:05:27,487
to steal your login credentials faster than a ninja in

99
00:05:27,487 --> 00:05:30,903
a dark alley. Once they have your username and password,

100
00:05:30,903 --> 00:05:34,318
they've got the keys to your digital kingdom. This isn't

101
00:05:34,318 --> 00:05:37,733
just about losing a Netflix account; it can lead to

102
00:05:37,733 --> 00:05:41,149
identity theft, drained bank accounts, or even your entire professional

103
00:05:41,149 --> 00:05:44,564
network being compromised. Always, always check the sender's email address

104
00:05:44,564 --> 00:05:47,979
and hover over links to see where they actually lead.

105
00:05:47,979 --> 00:05:51,394
If in doubt, go directly to the company's official website,

106
00:05:51,394 --> 00:05:54,810
don't click the link in the email. It's the oldest

107
00:05:54,810 --> 00:05:58,225
trick in the book, and yet, it still ensnares millions

108
00:05:58,225 --> 00:05:59,250
every single day.

109
00:05:59,250 --> 00:06:04,140
Number 5, The Fake Online Store or Social Media Ad.

110
00:06:04,140 --> 00:06:07,563
You’re scrolling through Instagram or Facebook, and suddenly, there it

111
00:06:07,563 --> 00:06:10,986
is: that designer bag you’ve been eyeing, but for 90%

112
00:06:10,986 --> 00:06:14,409
off! Or maybe it’s a brand-new gaming console for half

113
00:06:14,409 --> 00:06:17,833
the price. It seems too good to be true, and

114
00:06:17,833 --> 00:06:21,256
guess what? It absolutely is. These fake online stores pop

115
00:06:21,256 --> 00:06:24,679
up like mushrooms after rain, often using stolen product images,

116
00:06:24,679 --> 00:06:28,103
professional-looking websites, and incredibly tempting prices to lure you in.

117
00:06:28,103 --> 00:06:31,526
You click, you add to cart, you enter your credit

118
00:06:31,526 --> 00:06:34,949
card details, and then... nothing. Your order never arrives, your

119
00:06:34,949 --> 00:06:38,372
money is gone, and the website vanishes faster than my

120
00:06:38,372 --> 00:06:41,796
motivation on a Monday morning. Sometimes, if you're "lucky," you'll

121
00:06:41,796 --> 00:06:45,219
receive a cheap, knock-off version of what you ordered –

122
00:06:45,219 --> 00:06:48,642
like getting a potato sack when you expected a designer

123
00:06:48,642 --> 00:06:52,066
dress. These scams are particularly prevalent during holiday shopping seasons.

124
00:06:52,066 --> 00:06:55,489
Always check reviews, look for legitimate contact information, and be

125
00:06:55,489 --> 00:06:58,912
wary of prices that are unbelievably low. If a brand-new

126
00:06:58,912 --> 00:07:02,336
iPhone 15 is selling for $100, it’s not a bargain,

127
00:07:02,336 --> 00:07:04,390
it’s a trap, plain and simple.

128
00:07:04,390 --> 00:07:08,249
This one is a real heartbreaker, literally and financially. Imagine

129
00:07:08,249 --> 00:07:12,109
meeting the perfect person online – they're charming, attentive, handsome,

130
00:07:12,109 --> 00:07:15,968
and seem to understand you better than anyone ever has.

131
00:07:15,968 --> 00:07:19,828
They live far away, often in another country, which explains

132
00:07:19,828 --> 00:07:23,687
why you can't meet in person. After weeks or months

133
00:07:23,687 --> 00:07:27,547
of intense digital courtship, they confess their undying love. Then,

134
00:07:27,547 --> 00:07:31,407
the inevitable happens: an "emergency." Their business deal went south,

135
00:07:31,407 --> 00:07:35,266
a family member is sick, they need money for a

136
00:07:35,266 --> 00:07:39,126
flight to finally meet you, or they're in some kind

137
00:07:39,126 --> 00:07:42,985
of legal trouble. And because you're so deeply invested emotionally,

138
00:07:42,985 --> 00:07:46,845
you send the money. Then more money. And more. Before

139
00:07:46,845 --> 00:07:50,705
you know it, you've emptied your savings, taken out loans,

140
00:07:50,705 --> 00:07:54,564
and maxed out credit cards, all for someone who never

141
00:07:54,564 --> 00:07:58,424
existed outside of their meticulously crafted persona. The FBI reported

142
00:07:58,424 --> 00:08:02,283
that romance scams cost Americans over $1.3 billion in 2022

143
00:08:02,283 --> 00:08:06,143
alone. These scammers are psychological manipulators, playing on loneliness and

144
00:08:06,143 --> 00:08:10,002
the desire for connection. If your online sweetheart needs money

145
00:08:10,002 --> 00:08:13,862
for any reason, especially if you've never met them in

146
00:08:13,862 --> 00:08:17,722
person, slam on the brakes. It's not love, it's a

147
00:08:17,722 --> 00:08:18,880
very expensive illusion.

148
00:08:18,880 --> 00:08:23,250
Number 3, The Utility or Government Impersonation Threat.

149
00:08:23,250 --> 00:08:26,649
Your phone rings, or you get a text message, and

150
00:08:26,649 --> 00:08:30,049
it's from someone claiming to be from your gas company,

151
00:08:30,049 --> 00:08:33,448
electric company, or even the IRS. The message is always

152
00:08:33,448 --> 00:08:36,848
urgent and terrifying: "Your power will be shut off in

153
00:08:36,848 --> 00:08:40,247
30 minutes if you don't pay your overdue bill immediately!"

154
00:08:40,247 --> 00:08:43,647
or "You owe back taxes and a warrant has been

155
00:08:43,647 --> 00:08:47,046
issued for your arrest!" They demand payment right away, often

156
00:08:47,046 --> 00:08:50,446
insisting on gift cards, wire transfers, or cryptocurrency, because, you

157
00:08:50,446 --> 00:08:53,845
know, that's how legitimate government agencies and utility companies collect

158
00:08:53,845 --> 00:08:57,245
payments. Yeah, no. These scams rely on fear and urgency

159
00:08:57,245 --> 00:09:00,644
to bypass your common sense. No legitimate utility company will

160
00:09:00,644 --> 00:09:04,044
threaten immediate shut-off without multiple prior warnings, and they certainly

161
00:09:04,044 --> 00:09:07,443
won't demand payment via gift cards. The IRS will never

162
00:09:07,443 --> 00:09:10,843
call you out of the blue threatening arrest. They do

163
00:09:10,843 --> 00:09:14,242
everything by mail, and they're notoriously slow, not lightning-fast with

164
00:09:14,242 --> 00:09:17,642
threats. If you get one of these calls, hang up.

165
00:09:17,642 --> 00:09:21,041
If you're genuinely concerned, call the utility company or the

166
00:09:21,041 --> 00:09:24,441
IRS directly using the number on their official website or

167
00:09:24,441 --> 00:09:27,840
your bill, not the number the scammer gives you. Your

168
00:09:27,840 --> 00:09:31,240
money and your freedom are not decided by a prepaid

169
00:09:31,240 --> 00:09:31,580
Visa.

170
00:09:31,580 --> 00:09:34,873
Finding an affordable place to live is stressful enough, but

171
00:09:34,873 --> 00:09:38,166
then you stumble upon it: the perfect apartment. Great location,

172
00:09:38,166 --> 00:09:41,460
beautiful photos, unbelievably low rent. You contact the "landlord," who

173
00:09:41,460 --> 00:09:44,753
is always conveniently out of town or abroad, so they

174
00:09:44,753 --> 00:09:48,047
can't show you the place in person. They'll tell you

175
00:09:48,047 --> 00:09:51,340
to drive by, look through the windows, and if you

176
00:09:51,340 --> 00:09:54,634
like it, they'll send you a lease agreement. All you

177
00:09:54,634 --> 00:09:57,927
need to do is send the first month's rent and

178
00:09:57,927 --> 00:10:01,220
security deposit via wire transfer or some other non-traceable method

179
00:10:01,220 --> 00:10:04,514
to secure the place. You send the money, excited about

180
00:10:04,514 --> 00:10:07,807
your new digs, and then... crickets. The "landlord" vanishes, the

181
00:10:07,807 --> 00:10:11,101
ad disappears, and you realize you just sent thousands of

182
00:10:11,101 --> 00:10:14,394
dollars to someone who doesn't own the property, or perhaps

183
00:10:14,394 --> 00:10:17,688
the property doesn't even exist as a rental. These scammers

184
00:10:17,688 --> 00:10:20,981
often steal photos from real estate listings or even Google

185
00:10:20,981 --> 00:10:24,274
Street View. It's like finding a unicorn that promises to

186
00:10:24,274 --> 00:10:27,568
pay your bills, but only if you give it all

187
00:10:27,568 --> 00:10:30,861
your money first. Always, always insist on seeing the property

188
00:10:30,861 --> 00:10:34,155
in person, meeting the landlord, and never send money for

189
00:10:34,155 --> 00:10:36,790
a place you haven't physically toured or verified.

190
00:10:36,790 --> 00:10:40,440
Number 1, The Multi-Level Marketing (MLM) Scheme.

191
00:10:40,440 --> 00:10:44,112
This one might surprise you, but it’s arguably the most

192
00:10:44,112 --> 00:10:47,784
insidious and widespread "everyday scam" because it’s so effectively disguised

193
00:10:47,784 --> 00:10:51,456
as a legitimate business opportunity. You get invited to a

194
00:10:51,456 --> 00:10:55,129
"business presentation" by an enthusiastic friend or acquaintance. They talk

195
00:10:55,129 --> 00:10:58,801
about financial freedom, being your own boss, and how you

196
00:10:58,801 --> 00:11:02,473
can earn passive income by selling amazing products and, more

197
00:11:02,473 --> 00:11:06,146
importantly, recruiting others to do the same. You buy an

198
00:11:06,146 --> 00:11:09,818
expensive starter kit, attend endless motivational meetings, and spend your

199
00:11:09,818 --> 00:11:13,490
days hounding friends and family to buy products or join

200
00:11:13,490 --> 00:11:17,163
your "team." The truth? Statistics consistently show that over 99%

201
00:11:17,163 --> 00:11:20,835
of people in MLMs lose money, with the vast majority

202
00:11:20,835 --> 00:11:24,507
of revenue coming from recruiting new distributors, not selling actual

203
00:11:24,507 --> 00:11:28,180
products. It’s a pyramid scheme with a product attached to

204
00:11:28,180 --> 00:11:31,852
make it seem legal, like putting a fancy hat on

205
00:11:31,852 --> 00:11:35,524
a snake and calling it a fashion consultant. You're not

206
00:11:35,524 --> 00:11:39,197
building a business; you're just paying to be part of

207
00:11:39,197 --> 00:11:42,869
an elaborate system designed to enrich those at the very

208
00:11:42,869 --> 00:11:46,541
top. It preys on hope, ambition, and community, turning friendships

209
00:11:46,541 --> 00:11:50,214
into sales targets and leaving a trail of debt and

210
00:11:50,214 --> 00:11:53,886
broken dreams. If the primary way to earn money is

211
00:11:53,886 --> 00:11:56,090
by recruiting others, run, don't walk.

212
00:11:56,090 --> 00:11:59,571
That's all for today. I'll be making similar videos in

213
00:11:59,571 --> 00:12:01,660
the future. Subscribe to see them.

